# Bevcheck

A standalone prototype that automates part of a TTB (Alcohol and Tobacco Tax
and Trade Bureau) Certificate of Label Approval (COLA) review. Given one or more
photographs of an alcohol-beverage label plus the matching application data, it
extracts the label text with OCR and verifies that the mandatory fields are
present and consistent with the application and with 27 CFR (Parts 4, 5, 7, 16).

The core deliverable is a **deterministic verification engine** -- no LLM, no
generative model. For background and the full legal/design rationale, see
[`prelim-research.txt`](prelim-research.txt).

---

## Overview

- **What it checks** (per label): brand name, class/type designation, alcohol
  content (ABV + proof cross-check), net contents, name & address (producer /
  bottler / importer), country of origin (imports), the mandatory government
  health warning (presence, wording, caps), and the conditional disclosures
  (sulfites, aspartame, FD&C Yellow No. 5, cochineal/carmine, coloring,
  neutral spirits, age, state of distillation, and malt low/non-alcoholic
  designations).
- **How it reports**: a three-way verdict per label -- `Approved`,
  `Needs Correction`, or `Rejected` -- plus a per-criterion breakdown with the
  extracted value and a short reason.
- **Architecture**: one Go monolith (`cmd/Bevcheck` - `internal/server`) that
  orchestrates AWS Textract for OCR and runs every verification criterion
  concurrently. Single image = a batch of one; the same async job pipeline
  serves both.

