## Methods

The verification engine lives in `internal/server/verify.go` and is a
**conjunction of independent Boolean criteria** (research.txt SS7.6.9). Each
criterion is a pure function `(context) -> (status, reason)`:

- **OCR** (`internal/server/ocr.go`): AWS Textract `DetectDocumentText`
  (raw text, line-level confidence + bounding boxes), read in top-to-bottom
  order. Results are memoized by the SHA-256 of the image bytes in an
  in-memory LRU cache (`internal/server/ocr_cache.go`).
- **Normalization** (`verify.go`): three tiers, all *mechanical* (no model):
  - `collapseWS` -- collapse whitespace (preserve case/punctuation);
  - `normalizeWording` -- lowercase + collapse whitespace (warning wording);
  - `normalizeFuzzy` -- lowercase + collapse whitespace + strip punctuation
    (brand name, name/address, class/type, etc.).
  Phrase matching is word-boundary-aware so `"TOM"` never matches `"AUTOMATIC"`.
- **Verdict folding**: any `fail` - `Rejected`; else any `needs_correction` -
  `Needs Correction`; else `Approved`. A criterion can also return the
  non-blocking `human_confirm` flag (it never changes the verdict).
- **Concurrency**: criteria are independent given the OCR text, so they run in
  parallel goroutines after the single serial OCR step.
- **Robustness**: each criterion is wrapped in a `recover()`, so a panic in one
  check degrades to `needs_correction` instead of crashing the process.

### Fuzzy string matching (normalization + edit distance)

"Fuzzy" here is strictly **mechanical** -- normalization and edit distance, never
model-based similarity. It exists to separate *formatting noise* (which the
regulation tolerates) from *substantive deviation* (which it does not).

**Three normalization tiers** (`verify.go`), each building on the last:

| Function | Transformation | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `collapseWS` | `\s+` - single space (case & punctuation preserved) | feeding the criteria |
| `normalizeWording` | lowercase + `collapseWS` (punctuation & word order kept) | warning-wording compare |
| `normalizeFuzzy` | lowercase + `collapseWS` + strip punctuation (`[^\pL\pN]+` - space) | brand, name/address, class/type, age, etc. |

`normalizeFuzzy` is deliberately the loosest and is reserved for fields where
the CFR allows formatting variation -- so `"STONE'S THROW"`, `"Stone's Throw"`,
and `"stones throw"` all collapse to the same string. It does **not** touch word
order or spellings; it only removes case, whitespace, and punctuation
differences.

**Phrase matching is word-boundary-aware** (`containsPhrase`): the needle and
haystack are compared with surrounding spaces (`" "+norm+" "`), so a short
class/type like `"gin"` or a brand like `"TOM"` never matches inside a longer
word (`"engine"`, `"automatic"`). This keeps substring checks on short tokens
safe.

**Levenshtein edit distance** (`levenshtein`) is the classic DP
insert/delete/substitute metric (cost 1 each), implemented with a two-row
rolling buffer over runes (Unicode-aware, O(n) memory). It is used only where
exactness matters but OCR is allowed to misread a character: the government
warning.

**Warning-wording comparison** (`checkWarningWording`) is the one place the two
philosophies combine:

1. First try an **exact** substring match of the verbatim `regs.GovernmentWarning`
   after `normalizeWording` -- whitespace/case-insensitive but otherwise
   word-for-word, punctuation included.
2. If that fails, `containsNear(haystack, needle, 3)` slides a window of
   `len(needle) +- 3` across the OCR text and reports whether any window is
   within **3 edits**. A hit here is `needs_correction` (surfaced for human
   review), *not* a pass.
3. Anything farther than 3 edits is `fail`.

The tight edit-distance allowance exists only to absorb OCR character errors
(dropped/misread glyphs). It is small enough that a real wording change -- a
dropped `(2)`, a reordered token, "Government Warning" instead of
"GOVERNMENT WARNING" -- never silently passes; those land in the
`needs_correction`/`fail` bucket and are shown to the agent with a reason.

### The 20 criteria

| Criterion | What it checks |
| --- | --- |
| `ocr_confidence` | min line confidence >= 85% (else needs correction) |
| `brand_name` | normalized fuzzy phrase match vs. application |
| `class_type` | presence of a recognized class/type (controlled vocabulary); mismatch flagged |
| `alcohol_content` | parse ABV from legal formats, compare vs. expected within tolerance, cross-check proof = 2xABV |
| `net_contents` | parse volume to canonical mL, compare vs. expected (+-1%), standard-of-fill check |
| `name_address` | function phrase ("bottled by" / "imported by") + applicant name + address |
| `country_of_origin` | imports only: normalized compare vs. asserted origin |
| `government_warning_present` | "government warning" present |
| `government_warning_wording` | verbatim match vs. `regs.GovernmentWarning`; <=3 edit-distance - needs correction |
| `government_warning_prefix_caps` | "GOVERNMENT WARNING" prefix is uppercase |
| `government_warning_format` | bold/type-size/contrast -- `human_confirm` flag only |
| `sulfite_disclosure` | "contains sulfites" present/negation-aware |
| `aspartame_disclosure` | all-caps `PHENYLKETONURICS: CONTAINS PHENYLALANINE` |
| `fdc_yellow5_disclosure` | FD&C Yellow No. 5 disclosure (incl. `#5` variant) |
| `carmine_disclosure` | carmine/cochineal disclosure, negation-aware |
| `age_statement` | asserted age appears on label (word-boundary-aware) |
| `coloring_disclosure` | coloring/treatment disclosure (excludes bare "color" noun) |
| `neutral_spirits_disclosure` | neutral-spirits disclosure present |
| `state_of_distillation` | asserted state appears on label |
| `malt_alcohol_designation` | malt "non-alcoholic" (<0.5%) / "low alcohol" (<2.5%) thresholds |

Regulatory constants (the verbatim warning, aspartame statement, ABV
tolerances, class/type vocabularies) live in `internal/regs/`.

