## Assumptions & limitations

These are the implicit contracts the implementation relies on. Worth surfacing
explicitly in a review:

1. **Deterministic only -- no Bedrock LLM.** Every criterion is normalization,
   regex, or edit-distance. A generative criterion was considered and dropped
   (see research.txt SS5.5); class/type is resolved against a *finite* controlled
   vocabulary, and residual ambiguity degrades to `Needs Correction`.
2. **Warning format is not machine-verified.** Bold on the first two words,
   minimum type size by container volume, characters-per-inch, legibility, and
   contrasting background (27 CFR 16.22) are only surfaced as a non-blocking
   `human_confirm` flag -- the engine never claims it verified them. Pixel-level
   bold/size detection is unreliable without a scale reference (research.txt
   SS8.4).
3. **Field-of-vision / placement checks are out of scope.** Only presence and
   legibility are checked, not "same field of vision" geometry, which needs
   multiple images per container (research.txt SS8.9).
4. **Class/type vocabularies are a representative subset** of 27 CFR subpart I /
   subpart C (research.txt SS2.4.2). Matching is phrase/boundary-aware; an
   unlisted designation degrades to `Needs Correction`.
5. **Fuzzy vs. exact matching boundary** (research.txt SS8.2): brand/name/
   address/class-type are case/punctuation/whitespace-tolerant; the government
   warning is verbatim (whitespace-normalized) with a tight <=3 edit-distance
   allowance for OCR noise only.
6. **ABV/net-contents are "label-derived", not form fields.** The real 5100.31
   does not carry them; this prototype keeps them in the flat application object
   as optional *expected* values and otherwise derives them from the label and
   validates against the CFR (research.txt SS4.7).
7. **Disclosure scope** (research.txt SS8.11): the engine checks *presence/format*
   of a disclosure when the application asserts the substance, and flags the
   reverse (label discloses, form does not). It does not independently determine
   whether a formula *requires* a disclosure.
8. **OCR & latency**: Textract is on the critical path; the ~5 s budget is a
   design target, not a measured guarantee. Textract sync TPS/quotas are the
   binding constraint and must be measured in a real deployment (research.txt
   SS6.7/8.6).
